    <div class="sheet">
    <section class="section">
      <h2>President's preface</h2>
      <p class="confirm">To confirm: Alexar to write or approve this. For now it's Alexar's own opening from the 2025 LUV report, which covers the calendar year rather than this financial year.</p>
      <p class="lead">Around a year ago, a small team put their hands up to take an active role in defining and shaping our local Linux community here in Victoria. It is my honour as the lead organiser to share our journey, what we tried as a team and accomplished as a community and what we learned. We are hoping this will be helpful for our future partners, volunteers and communities who are in similar positions as we are.</p>
      <p class="lead">The 2025 LUV committee was formed at a long overdue AGM in an entirely different world than the previous AGM back in 2019. Looking back, the work of the committee in 2025 can be described as re-building organisational infrastructure as well as revisiting the identity of the group and the broader community.</p>
      <p class="signoff"><strong>Alexar Pendashteh</strong><br>President, Linux Victoria</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>The year at a glance</h2>
      <p class="lead">Linux Victoria spent this financial year turning a small volunteer team into a regular rhythm: a monthly Town Hall, an ongoing conversation about Linux and AI, and a run of bigger community days.</p>
      <p>We took Linux out to Morwell and Canberra, brought the Penguin Picnic back after six years, and returned Software Freedom Day to Melbourne PC User Group. Document Freedom Day and Hardware Freedom Day joined the calendar in 2026, the second of them a first for Australia and a first collaboration with Melbourne Tech Guilds.</p>
      <p>The year finished with the Town Hall moving to Kathleen Syme Library in Carlton, and a third season of LAIT Night getting underway.</p>
    </section>
